# Forearm mechanics in 3D reasoning practice

Six original formative concepts bind twelve existing left/right whole-muscle
representations. The prompts distinguish middle versus distal phalangeal flexion,
second versus third versus fifth metacarpal wrist attachments, and common finger
versus additional index extension. Flexor digitorum profundus is represented as
one whole muscle; the question makes no claim about individual finger or nerve
territories. The existing reason-mode choices, feedback and retry path are used.

The University of Washington Radiology muscle atlas was checked on 23 October
2026 for the named attachments and actions: [flexor digitorum superficialis](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/flexor-digitorum-superficialis),
[flexor digitorum profundus](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/flexor-digitorum-profundus),
[extensor carpi radialis brevis](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/extensor-carpi-radialis-brevis),
[extensor carpi radialis longus](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/extensor-carpi-radialis-longus),
[extensor carpi ulnaris](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/extensor-carpi-ulnaris),
[extensor digitorum](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/extensor-digitorum) and
[extensor indicis](https://rad.uw.edu/muscle-atlas/extensor-indicis).
Its illustrations are copyrighted and are not licensed into this batch. Only
factual citations and newly written questions/explanations are used. No image,
table, proprietary question, scan, geometry, compartment assertion or patient
diagnosis is imported. Source holds and licensing remain in force.

